// Original educational content (English edition). Not ISACA text.
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN = window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN || [];
window.COBIT_CONTROLS_EN.push(...
[
{"objective":"BAI01","purpose":"The organization’s digital transformation and IT investments are mostly delivered as programs made up of multiple interdependent projects. Initiating, monitoring and closing a program in a structured way makes visible whether the budget spent is being converted into the expected business benefit. Weak program management results in cost overruns, delays and the continuation of investments that do not deliver value.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether programs are initiated on the basis of an approved business case, and whether benefits and risks are regularly monitored and reported to management throughout the life cycle. Particular focus is placed on the documentation of decisions taken at stage gates and on the assessment of benefit realization at closure.","keyRisks":["Programs initiated without an approved business case, or continued despite not delivering benefits","Delays and cost overruns due to unmanaged dependencies and resource conflicts","Late identification of program risks and information security requirements"],"practices":[
{"code":"BAI01.01","name":"Maintain a standard approach for program management.","title":"Keeping a standard, up-to-date program management methodology","expectation":"The organization has an approved and up-to-date methodology for programs; it defines roles such as sponsor, program manager and steering committee, as well as stage gates, mandatory documentation and reporting arrangements. The methodology has an owner and is reviewed at intervals defined by the organization or on the basis of lessons learned. Information security and privacy assessments are embedded as an integral step of the approach.","design":"Assess whether the methodology covers governance decisions, stage transitions and benefit tracking, and whether it embeds security requirements into the project life cycle.","operating":"Obtain the complete list of programs active during the period from the portfolio management tool; for programs selected based on size and risk, inspect whether the outputs required by the methodology were actually produced.","evidence":["Approved program management methodology and version history","Template set (program charter, stage gate checklist, status report)","Methodology review records and updates based on lessons learned","Inventory of programs active during the period"],"findings":["A methodology exists but has not been updated for a long time and does not reflect the practices of teams working in an agile manner.","Some programs progressed to the next stage without obtaining the stage gate approval required by the methodology."],"iso":["5.8"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.02","name":"Initiate a program.","title":"Approving and authorizing a program before it starts","expectation":"Each program is based on a business case setting out expected benefits, costs, assumptions and key risks, and is formally approved by an authorized investment committee or sponsor. The sponsor, program manager and scope are clearly defined, and the program’s link to strategic objectives and the portfolio is documented. No budget is used and no resources are allocated before approval is obtained.","design":"Assess whether the initiation process includes an approval authority matrix, minimum business case content and a portfolio alignment check.","operating":"Validate the completeness of the list of programs initiated during the period by reconciling it with budget and procurement records; for selected programs, inspect whether the approval date precedes the first expenditure and whether the approver was authorized.","evidence":["Approved business case and program charter","Investment committee or steering committee decision minutes","Approval authority matrix","Dates of first expenditure and purchase order"],"findings":["Benefits are not quantified in the business case and therefore cannot be measured subsequently.","In one program, expenditure began before formal approval and the approval was completed retrospectively."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.03","name":"Manage stakeholder engagement.","title":"Identifying stakeholders and keeping them engaged","expectation":"Program stakeholders such as business units, IT, information security, compliance and suppliers have been identified and analyzed according to their level of influence and interest, and a communication plan has been prepared. Regular meetings, status updates and feedback channels with stakeholders are operated; critical decisions and objections are recorded. The stakeholder list is updated as the program progresses.","design":"Assess whether the stakeholder analysis also covers control functions such as information security, compliance and operations, and whether the communication plan specifies responsibilities, frequency and channels.","operating":"For selected programs, inspect meeting minutes and records to confirm that the meetings and reports set out in the communication plan took place and that stakeholder feedback was recorded and responded to.","evidence":["Stakeholder analysis or stakeholder map","Program communication plan","Steering committee and stakeholder meeting minutes","Stakeholder feedback and decision logs"],"findings":["Information security and operations teams were not identified as stakeholders; as a result, their requirements emerged at a late stage.","A communication plan was prepared, but meetings were held irregularly and no minutes were kept."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.04","name":"Develop and maintain the program plan.","title":"Building a program plan and keeping it current","expectation":"The program plan presents together the scope, deliverables, inter-project dependencies, milestones, resource and budget needs, and the benefit realization schedule. The plan is retained as an approved baseline; significant deviations in scope, duration or cost are subject to formal change approval based on thresholds defined by the organization. The program manager is responsible for keeping the plan up to date.","design":"Assess whether the plan includes dependencies and the benefit schedule, and whether approval thresholds are defined for changes to the baseline plan.","operating":"For selected programs, compare plan versions to confirm that changes above the threshold were approved and that the current plan is consistent with actual status.","evidence":["Approved baseline program plan and subsequent versions","Dependency and milestone list","Plan change requests and approvals","Resource and budget plan"],"findings":["Program dates have changed in practice, but because the baseline plan was not updated, deviations cannot be reported.","Inter-project dependencies are not included in the plan; consequently, a delay in one project unexpectedly affected other projects."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.05","name":"Launch and execute the program.","title":"Mobilizing and running the program and directing its resources","expectation":"At program start, the team, governance structure, working environments and tools are put in place, and project managers are given their responsibilities and authorities in writing. During execution, resource allocation, supplier contributions and deliverable handovers are managed by comparison against the plan. The implementation of information security requirements in projects is checked at stage gates.","design":"Assess whether roles and authorities, resource allocation rules and security checks at stage gates are defined.","operating":"For selected projects, inspect kick-off meeting records, resource assignments and stage gate checklists to verify that execution is consistent with the approved plan and methodology.","evidence":["Program kick-off meeting records and RACI matrix","Resource allocation records","Stage gate checklists including security items","Supplier contracts and work orders"],"findings":["Individuals assigned to critical roles appear as full-time on more than one program during the same period; the resource conflict has not been managed.","Security items on the stage gate checklist are ticked, but no supporting documentation is available."],"iso":["5.8"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.06","name":"Monitor, control and report on the program outcomes.","title":"Tracking program performance and benefit realization","expectation":"The program is monitored regularly through cost, schedule, scope, risk and benefit indicators, and results are reported to the steering committee at a frequency defined by the organization. Thresholds and escalation paths are defined for deviations; corrective actions are tracked with an owner and target date. The business case is re-evaluated when significant changes occur.","design":"Assess whether reporting covers not only activity progress but also benefit realization, and whether deviation thresholds are defined so as to trigger escalation.","operating":"Test the accuracy of status reports produced during the period by comparing them with source data such as the finance system and the portfolio tool; for threshold breaches, inspect whether escalation and action records exist.","evidence":["Periodic program status reports and dashboards","Steering committee minutes","Actual cost reports from the finance system","Corrective action tracking log"],"findings":["Although all indicators in the status reports appear favorable, finance records show a budget overrun.","The business case was not re-evaluated despite significant scope changes."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.07","name":"Manage program quality.","title":"Planning and assuring program quality","expectation":"The program has a quality plan that defines acceptance criteria, quality reviews, responsibilities and the quality records to be maintained. Quality reviews and independent assurance activities are performed at points defined by the organization; identified nonconformities are tracked until closed. Quality results provide input to stage gate decisions.","design":"Assess whether the quality plan includes measurable acceptance criteria and independent review points.","operating":"For selected programs, verify through quality records that planned quality reviews were performed and that findings were recorded and closed.","evidence":["Program quality plan","Quality review and quality assurance reports","Nonconformity and action records with evidence of closure","Quality inputs used in stage gate decisions"],"findings":["The quality plan contains generic statements; acceptance criteria are not defined at deliverable level.","A stage transition was approved before findings raised in the quality review were closed."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.08","name":"Manage program risk.","title":"Identifying and managing program risks","expectation":"The program risk register includes technical, financial, supplier, information security and compliance risks, together with an owner, a likelihood-impact assessment and response actions. The risk register is reviewed at a frequency defined by the organization and after significant events; risks exceeding risk appetite are escalated to senior management. Program risks are assessed consistently with the enterprise risk management method.","design":"Assess whether the risk management method is aligned with the enterprise risk methodology and whether it covers information security risks.","operating":"Inspect the update history of the risk registers of selected programs; trace whether issues that materialized had previously been identified as risks and whether response actions were implemented.","evidence":["Program risk register and update history","Risk assessment method","Escalation records and committee decisions","Evidence of completion of risk response actions"],"findings":["The risk register was created at program initiation but has not been updated since.","Information security and data privacy risks are not included in the program risk register."],"iso":["5.8"],"clauses":["6.1"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI01.09","name":"Close a program.","title":"Formally closing a program and assessing the benefits achieved","expectation":"A program is removed from the active portfolio through a formal closure decision when the expected benefits have been achieved or when it becomes clear that they will not be achieved. At closure, the status of benefit realization, outstanding work and its owners, elements handed over to operations and lessons learned are documented. A post-implementation benefit review is performed at a time defined by the organization.","design":"Assess whether the closure process includes benefit assessment, handover to operations and removal of temporary access granted during the project.","operating":"List all programs closed during the period; for selected programs, inspect the closure report, handover approval and evidence that temporary access granted to the project team and suppliers was removed.","evidence":["Program closure report and approval","Benefit realization assessment","Lessons learned log","Records of handover to operations and closure of temporary access"],"findings":["Programs that have effectively been completed continue to appear as open in the portfolio, and costs continue to be accrued.","Temporary access granted to the project team and suppliers was not removed after closure."],"iso":["5.18"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"BAI02","purpose":"Whether a solution will meet the business need depends largely on how accurately and completely the requirements are defined. Identifying non-functional requirements such as security, application controls, data quality and performance early, alongside functional requirements, prevents costly later rework and control gaps. Evaluating alternatives ensures that the investment is made in the most suitable option.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether requirements have been approved by the business owner and whether control requirements such as input validation, authorization and audit trail are included in a clear and testable form. The auditor also checks whether feasibility and requirements risk assessments were performed before decisions were made.","keyRisks":["Solutions that do not meet the business need due to incomplete or ambiguous requirements","Failure to define security and application control requirements","Development starting without business owner approval, and uncontrolled scope creep"],"practices":[
{"code":"BAI02.01","name":"Define and maintain business functional and technical requirements.","title":"Documenting business, functional and technical requirements, including control requirements","expectation":"Requirements are documented so as to cover business processes, functional behavior, technical constraints and control expectations such as information security, data protection, audit trail and access authorization. Each requirement has a source, priority and acceptance criterion, and is linked to design and testing through a traceability matrix. The requirement set is maintained under version control in a central tool.","design":"Assess whether the requirements template includes security and application control requirements as mandatory fields and whether traceability is defined.","operating":"For projects and releases deployed to production during the period and selected on a risk basis, inspect requirements documents or backlog items to verify the presence of security requirements and acceptance criteria and their linkage to test records.","evidence":["Requirements document or backlog of user stories with acceptance criteria","Security and privacy requirements checklist","Requirements–test traceability matrix","Version history in the requirements tool"],"findings":["Role-based authorization and audit trail expectations are not defined in the requirements.","User stories without acceptance criteria were marked as complete without being tested."],"iso":["8.26","5.8"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI02.02","name":"Perform a feasibility study and formulate alternative solutions.","title":"Assessing feasibility and comparing alternative solutions before deciding","expectation":"For significant investments, options such as an off-the-shelf package, in-house development, a cloud service or extension of an existing system are compared in terms of cost, benefit, technical fit, security, supplier dependency and compliance. The rationale for the selected option is documented and the decision is approved by the authorized body. The depth of the feasibility study is scaled to the size of the investment using criteria defined by the organization.","design":"Assess whether the feasibility template includes criteria for comparing alternatives (including security, cloud and supplier risks) and decision approval.","operating":"For selected investments, inspect whether feasibility reports were prepared before the decision date, whether alternatives were genuinely evaluated and whether approval was obtained.","evidence":["Feasibility report and alternatives comparison table","Cost-benefit analysis","Decision approval or committee minutes","Supplier or cloud service risk assessment"],"findings":["The feasibility report addresses a single option only; alternatives were not evaluated.","Data residency and supplier dependency risks were not included in the assessment when selecting a cloud-based solution."],"iso":["5.23"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI02.03","name":"Manage requirements risk.","title":"Identifying and mitigating risks arising from requirements","expectation":"Data integrity, integration, unauthorized access, performance and compliance risks arising from the requirements, assumptions and proposed solution are identified and prioritized, and mitigating measures are fed back into the requirement set. Security and privacy impact assessments are performed for solutions that process personal or critical data. The risk assessment is updated when requirements change significantly.","design":"Assess whether the circumstances in which a risk assessment is mandatory (such as critical data, external access or new technology) are defined, and whether the process ensures that results are translated into requirements.","operating":"For selected projects, trace that the risk assessment was performed before design and that the identified measures are reflected in requirement and test records.","evidence":["Requirements and solution risk assessment","Security or privacy impact assessment","Requirement records linked to risk mitigations","Risk assessment update records"],"findings":["No privacy impact assessment was performed for an application that processes personal data.","Measures recommended in the risk assessment were not carried into the requirement set and were not tested."],"iso":["8.26","5.34","5.8"],"clauses":["6.1","8.2"],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI02.04","name":"Obtain approval of requirements and solutions.","title":"Obtaining business owner sign-off on requirements and solutions","expectation":"Requirements and the proposed solution are formally approved by the business sponsor or product owner at key stages defined by the organization, with input obtained from relevant functions such as security, architecture and operations. Approvals are dated and traceable; changes after approval are subject to a controlled change process. In agile environments, approval is evidenced through backlog prioritization and sprint acceptance records.","design":"Assess whether approval points, approvers and the functions whose input is required are defined, and whether a control exists to prevent unapproved development.","operating":"For selected projects, compare approval dates with development start dates and verify that the approver is included in the authorization list.","evidence":["Requirements document signed or approved in the system","Product owner acceptance records in the agile tool","Security and architecture review records","Approval authority list"],"findings":["Requirements approval was given only by the IT project manager; there is no business owner approval.","Development started before requirements approval, and the approval record was completed retrospectively."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"}
]},
{"objective":"BAI03","purpose":"This objective covers turning approved requirements into secure, tested and sustainable solutions. Controls embedded in the design, development, acquisition, configuration and testing phases directly determine the accuracy and security of the applications that support business processes. Weak development discipline leads to security vulnerabilities that are costly to remediate later and to inaccurate financial or operational data.","auditFocus":"Within ITGC scope, the auditor tests whether development and test environments are segregated from production, whether code review and testing activities are evidenced, and whether user acceptance is obtained before deployment to production. The auditor also focuses on the evaluation of acquired components, control over requirement changes and the protection of personal data used in testing.","keyRisks":["Insufficiently tested or unapproved code being deployed to production","Security vulnerabilities arising from non-compliance with secure coding and architecture principles","Use of unmasked production data in test environments"],"practices":[
{"code":"BAI03.01","name":"Design high-level solutions.","title":"Defining the overall solution architecture and having it reviewed","expectation":"Based on approved requirements, the solution’s architecture, main components, data flows, integrations and impact on business processes are documented. The design is aligned with enterprise architecture principles and with security architecture principles such as defense in depth, least privilege and secure defaults, and is reviewed by an architecture board or equivalent body. Traceability of the design to requirements is maintained.","design":"Assess whether the high-level design is subject to architecture and security review and whether the design template includes data flows and trust boundaries.","operating":"For selected projects, inspect architecture board decisions and design documents; verify that findings from the security review were reflected in the design.","evidence":["High-level solution and architecture design document","Data flow diagram and trust boundaries","Architecture board decisions","Security architecture review report"],"findings":["Integration flows with external systems are not shown in the design; data transfer security was not assessed.","The design was approved without an architecture board review."],"iso":["8.27","8.25"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.02","name":"Design detailed solution components.","title":"Specifying detailed components and built-in application controls","expectation":"Screens, data structures, interfaces, business rules and application controls such as input validation, processing controls, authorization checks, error handling and logging are designed in detail. The design is at a level that development and test teams can use directly and is reviewed by business analysts and security specialists. In agile approaches, detail is added incrementally, but control design is still recorded.","design":"Assess whether the detailed design standard makes application controls and the design of logging and audit trails mandatory.","operating":"For selected components, inspect design documents or technical task records to verify that the defined application controls are included in the design and that a review record exists.","evidence":["Detailed design documents or technical task records","Interface and data model specifications","Application controls and logging design","Design review records"],"findings":["Reconciliation and error handling controls were not designed for an interface that transfers financial data.","The detailed design exists only in code comments; there is no evidence that it was reviewed."],"iso":["8.27","8.26","8.15"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.03","name":"Develop solution components.","title":"Building code securely with peer review and segregated environments","expectation":"Development is performed in environments segregated from production and in accordance with the organization’s secure coding standard; source code is kept in a version control system, and code review and automated security analysis are applied before merging into the main branch. Developers have no authority to make changes directly in production. Access to source code is restricted on a role basis.","design":"Assess whether branch protection rules, mandatory code review, static analysis and environment segregation are configured so as to prevent unapproved code from progressing.","operating":"Obtain the complete population of changes merged into the main branch during the period from the version control system; for a risk-based selection, inspect whether the reviewer differs from the code author and whether analysis findings were addressed; check developers’ production access using read-only access listings.","evidence":["Secure coding standard","Branch protection settings and pull request records","Static code analysis and dependency scanning reports","Environment segregation and production access lists"],"findings":["Branch protection is disabled in some repositories, allowing merges without review.","High-severity findings identified by static analysis were left open without a documented acceptance rationale."],"iso":["8.25","8.28","8.31","8.4"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.04","name":"Procure solution components.","title":"Acquiring software, services and components with security and contractual safeguards","expectation":"Purchased software, cloud services, libraries or hardware components are selected according to approved requirements and architecture standards; the supplier’s security capability, licensing terms and support commitments are assessed. Contracts include provisions on security, privacy, right to audit, data return and service levels. Approved-source and license controls are also applied to open-source components.","design":"Assess whether the acquisition process covers technical and security assessment, contractual security provisions and control over open-source components.","operating":"For selected IT purchases during the period, inspect the supplier assessment, contract provisions and technical approval records; compare the software component inventory with license records.","evidence":["Supplier assessment and selection report","Contracts including security and privacy provisions","Technical and architectural fit approval","Software bill of materials (SBOM) and license records"],"findings":["The contract for a SaaS solution does not include data return or right-to-audit provisions.","No license or known vulnerability checks were performed for open-source libraries used in the project."],"iso":["5.19","5.20","5.23"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.05","name":"Build solutions.","title":"Installing, hardening and integrating solution components","expectation":"Application and infrastructure components are installed according to approved hardening standards, such as disabling default accounts and unnecessary services, and are integrated with business processes. Configurations are documented and recorded in the configuration management database; infrastructure as code (IaC) and automation are used where possible. Authentication and data transfer security are ensured at integration points.","design":"Assess whether installation and configuration are based on approved security baseline configurations and whether the creation of configuration records is mandatory.","operating":"For selected new systems, compare the actual configuration against the baseline using read-only reports or configuration compliance scan outputs, and verify the existence of a CMDB record.","evidence":["Security baseline configuration (hardening) standards","Installation and configuration records or infrastructure code","Configuration compliance scan reports","CMDB records"],"findings":["Default administrator accounts were left enabled on newly installed servers.","Newly installed systems were not recorded in the CMDB; asset ownership remains unclear."],"iso":["8.9","8.27"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.06","name":"Perform quality assurance (QA).","title":"Running independent quality assurance throughout development","expectation":"A quality assurance plan is applied throughout the development process to verify compliance with standards, design and requirements; the quality assurance role is sufficiently independent of the development team. Code quality metrics, reviews and process compliance checks are recorded, and deviations are tracked until closed. Quality results provide input to the go-live decision.","design":"Assess whether the quality assurance plan defines independence, scope and quality gate criteria.","operating":"For selected releases, inspect quality gate results, QA review reports and deviation records to verify that releases not meeting the criteria did not progress without exception approval.","evidence":["Quality assurance plan","Quality gate results in the CI/CD pipeline","QA review reports","Deviation and exception approval records"],"findings":["Releases were progressed manually even though quality gate thresholds were not met, and no rationale was recorded.","QA activities rely on the development team’s own self-assessment; no independent review is performed."],"iso":["8.29","8.25"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.07","name":"Prepare for solution testing.","title":"Planning tests and preparing protected test environments and data","expectation":"A test plan exists that covers unit, integration, system, security, performance and user acceptance testing, and defines responsibilities, entry and exit criteria and the test data approach. Test environments are sufficiently representative of production but segregated from it; where personal or sensitive data is required in test data, masking or anonymization is applied. The test plan is traceable to requirements.","design":"Assess whether the test plan covers security testing and user acceptance, and whether test data protection rules are defined.","operating":"For selected projects, inspect the test plan and test environment configuration; verify through masking job records or read-only inspection that sensitive data is protected, without the auditor copying personal data as evidence.","evidence":["Test strategy and test plan","Test environment inventory and access lists","Test data masking procedure and job records","Requirements-to-test scenario mapping"],"findings":["Unmasked customer data is being used in the user acceptance testing environment.","Security and performance testing are not included in the test plan."],"iso":["8.31","8.33","8.29"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.08","name":"Execute solution testing.","title":"Executing tests, resolving defects and obtaining user acceptance before go-live","expectation":"Tests are executed according to plan; results and defects are recorded in a test management tool, and no deployment to production takes place until critical defects are closed or a risk acceptance is documented. Security testing such as static and dynamic analysis or penetration testing is performed in proportion to risk. User acceptance is formally given by the business owner and test evidence is retained.","design":"Assess whether go-live criteria require the absence of open critical defects and business owner acceptance.","operating":"Extract the complete list of changes deployed to production during the period from release and change records; for a risk-based selection, inspect test results, open defect status and whether user acceptance approval preceded the go-live date.","evidence":["Test execution records and results reports","Defect records and closure status","User acceptance testing approvals","Security test reports and remediation tracking"],"findings":["For some releases, user acceptance approval was obtained after the go-live date.","The system was deployed to production without remediating high-risk vulnerabilities identified in penetration testing, and no risk acceptance was documented."],"iso":["8.29"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.09","name":"Manage changes to requirements.","title":"Controlling changes to approved requirements","expectation":"Additions, changes and cancellations to approved requirements are recorded; their cost, schedule, security and testing impacts are assessed, and they are approved by an authorized person. The status of all requirements, including rejected requests, is traceable. Approved changes are reflected in design, testing and documentation.","design":"Assess whether the requirements change process includes impact analysis, approval authority and traceability updates.","operating":"For selected projects, inspect the change history in the requirements tool; verify that requirements added after the approved baseline have an approval record and related test evidence.","evidence":["Requirement change requests and impact analyses","Change approval records","Audit trail of the requirements tool","Updated traceability matrix"],"findings":["Requirements added within a sprint were developed without product owner approval or impact analysis.","Changed requirements were not reflected in test scenarios, and the related functions were therefore not tested."],"iso":["8.32"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"},
{"code":"BAI03.10","name":"Maintain solutions.","title":"Patching, upgrading and keeping solutions supported over time","expectation":"A maintenance plan exists for application and infrastructure components covering patching, version upgrades, end-of-life tracking and technical debt management. Solutions are reviewed at intervals defined by the organization against business needs and known vulnerabilities; maintenance activities go through the change management process. Migration is planned in advance for components that will fall out of support.","design":"Assess whether the maintenance plan covers end-of-life tracking, security patches and periodic fitness-for-purpose reviews.","operating":"Compare the application and infrastructure inventory with vendor end-of-support dates; for selected maintenance activities, inspect whether they match change records and whether planned reviews were performed.","evidence":["Maintenance plan and technology roadmap","Software and hardware inventory including end-of-support dates","Patch and version upgrade records","Change requests for maintenance activities"],"findings":["A critical application runs on a database version for which the vendor has ended support, and no migration plan exists.","Some maintenance activities were performed without a change record being raised."],"iso":["8.8","8.32"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"change"},
{"code":"BAI03.11","name":"Define IT products and services and maintain the service portfolio.","title":"Defining IT services and keeping the service catalog and portfolio current","expectation":"New or changed IT services are defined with their scope, owner, service level options, support model and dependencies, and are recorded in the service catalog and portfolio. Service definitions are agreed with and approved by business units, and are updated at the same time as go-live. Retired services are removed from the portfolio in a controlled manner.","design":"Assess whether a catalog entry and a service level definition are mandatory before new services go live.","operating":"Compare services deployed or retired during the period with the service catalog; for selected services, verify that owner, service level and approval information is present.","evidence":["Service catalog and portfolio records","Service definition documents and service level options","Business unit approvals","Service onboarding and retirement records"],"findings":["A newly deployed service is not included in the catalog and no service owner has been defined.","Retired services continue to appear as active in the catalog."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI03.12","name":"Design solutions based on the defined development methodology.","title":"Applying a defined development methodology with minimum controls for every delivery approach","expectation":"The organization defines a development methodology specifying which approach (waterfall, agile or hybrid) is to be used according to the nature of the project; under every approach, minimum controls such as requirements approval, code review, testing, acceptance and segregation of duties are preserved. Teams document the approach they select, and controls are applied automatically in DevOps pipelines as far as possible. The methodology is reviewed at intervals defined by the organization.","design":"Assess whether the methodology defines a minimum control set for the different approaches and whether it also applies to outsourced development.","operating":"For selected projects, inspect the approach and toolchain used and verify that the minimum controls are actually applied, using evidence such as CI/CD pipeline configuration and sprint records.","evidence":["Approved software development life cycle (SDLC) methodology","Record of the approach selected for each project","CI/CD pipeline configuration and control steps","Methodology requirements in outsourced development contracts"],"findings":["Minimum controls are not defined for agile teams; these teams do not retain test and acceptance evidence.","The outsourced development supplier’s compliance with the organization’s methodology is not monitored."],"iso":["8.25","8.30"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"sdlc"}
]},
{"objective":"BAI04","purpose":"The uninterrupted operation of business processes at an acceptable speed depends on infrastructure and application resources being planned to meet demand. Availability and capacity management makes it possible to anticipate outages that may arise from sudden load increases, growth and new services, and to balance cost and performance. This area is the preventive, day-to-day operational side of business continuity.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether availability and performance targets are defined for critical services, whether capacity is continuously monitored and whether threshold breaches are addressed in a timely manner. The auditor also checks whether capacity plans are linked to business forecasts and new projects, and whether the root causes of recurring performance issues are resolved.","keyRisks":["Outages or serious degradation of critical services due to insufficient capacity","Incorrect prioritization due to resource planning performed without knowledge of business impact","Monitoring thresholds not defined and alerts not acted upon"],"practices":[
{"code":"BAI04.01","name":"Assess current availability, performance and capacity and create a baseline.","title":"Measuring current availability, performance and capacity to establish baselines","expectation":"Usage and performance data are collected for critical services and the supporting server, storage, network, database and cloud resources in order to establish baselines. Baselines are compared with service level targets and refreshed at intervals defined by the organization or after significant architectural changes. The measurement scope is consistent with the asset inventory.","design":"Assess whether baselines are defined so as to cover all critical services and whether they are linked to service level targets.","operating":"Test completeness by comparing the critical services list with the coverage in the monitoring tool; for selected components, inspect whether baseline reports are current.","evidence":["Capacity and performance baseline reports","Critical services and components list","Coverage and asset list in the monitoring tool","Service level targets"],"findings":["Some critical database servers are not within monitoring scope and no baseline has been established for them.","Baselines were not updated following the cloud migration."],"iso":["8.6"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI04.02","name":"Assess business impact.","title":"Assessing the business impact of outages and performance degradation","expectation":"Dependencies between critical business processes and the IT services and resources that support them are mapped. The financial, operational, customer and compliance impacts of unavailability or performance degradation are assessed in order to prioritize services, and this assessment is consistent with business continuity analyses. Results are agreed with and approved by business unit owners.","design":"Assess whether the impact assessment includes service–resource dependencies and business unit approval, and whether consistency with the business impact analysis (BIA) is ensured.","operating":"For selected critical processes, inspect the dependency map and impact rating; compare the priorities in the BIA, the CMDB and capacity plans for consistency with one another.","evidence":["Service–process–resource dependency map","Business impact analysis (BIA) results","Service criticality classification","Business unit approvals"],"findings":["The criticality ranking used in capacity planning does not match the current business impact analysis.","Services that depend on shared infrastructure components have not been mapped; as a result, single points of failure have not been identified."],"iso":["5.30"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI04.03","name":"Plan for new or changed service requirements.","title":"Planning capacity for new or changing service demand","expectation":"The impact of new projects, business growth, campaigns and regulatory changes on capacity and availability is forecast in advance and reflected in the capacity plan. The plan is updated in periods defined by the organization, linked to the budgeting process and includes redundancy and scalability needs. Performance requirements for new solutions are defined at the design stage.","design":"Assess whether project and change processes trigger a capacity impact assessment and whether the capacity plan is based on business volume forecasts.","operating":"For significant projects deployed to production during the period, inspect whether a capacity impact assessment was performed and whether its results are reflected in the capacity plan.","evidence":["Capacity plan and update history","Capacity and performance impact assessments for projects","Business volume forecasts obtained from business units","Budget records for capacity investments"],"findings":["A new digital channel was launched without a capacity assessment, and performance issues occurred in the first weeks.","The capacity plan is based solely on historical trends; business units’ growth forecasts were not taken into account."],"iso":["8.6","8.14"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI04.04","name":"Monitor and review availability and capacity.","title":"Monitoring availability and capacity and reviewing trends","expectation":"Critical components are monitored with automated tools that have defined alert thresholds, and threshold breaches are passed to the incident management process. Availability and capacity trend reports are prepared at a frequency defined by the organization and reviewed with service owners. Deviations from baselines are analyzed and the necessary actions are initiated.","design":"Assess whether monitoring coverage and thresholds are aligned with critical services and whether alerts are linked to a process with a defined owner.","operating":"Obtain threshold breach alerts for the period from the monitoring tool; for a selection, inspect whether they were converted into incident records and addressed in a timely manner, and whether periodic trend reports were prepared and reviewed.","evidence":["Monitoring tool threshold configurations (read-only screenshot or export)","Alert and incident records","Periodic availability and capacity trend reports","Service review meeting minutes"],"findings":["Disk utilization alerts are closed automatically and no incident record is created.","Availability reports are prepared, but there is no evidence that they are reviewed with service owners."],"iso":["8.6","8.16"],"clauses":[],"itgc":"operations"},
{"code":"BAI04.05","name":"Investigate and address availability, performance and capacity issues.","title":"Investigating and permanently resolving availability, performance and capacity issues","expectation":"Identified performance and capacity issues are logged and prioritized by impact, and root cause analysis is performed so that permanent solutions such as resource increases, optimization or architectural changes are implemented. Emergency capacity interventions are also subject to change management rules. Recurring issues are referred to problem management, and the effectiveness of the solution is verified before closure.","design":"Assess whether rules are defined to ensure that issues are directed to root cause analysis and problem management and that corrective actions go through the change process.","operating":"List the incident and problem records arising from performance and capacity issues during the period; for a selection, inspect the root cause analysis, the related change record and whether the issue recurred.","evidence":["Incident and problem records arising from performance and capacity issues","Root cause analysis reports","Change records for corrective actions","Post-closure verification and monitoring results"],"findings":["Recurring slowdowns in the same application are resolved temporarily each time by a restart; no root cause analysis has been performed.","Emergency capacity increases were made without a change record being raised."],"iso":["8.6"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""}
]},
{"objective":"BAI05","purpose":"A technically successful solution does not deliver the expected benefit if it is not adopted by users. Organizational change management ensures that new systems, processes and ways of working are understood, owned and embedded. This area also matters for the control environment: users who have not been adequately informed apply new controls incorrectly or resort to workarounds.","auditFocus":"The auditor examines whether, for significant transformations, a change readiness analysis, senior management sponsorship, and communication and training plans exist and have been implemented. The auditor also checks whether the level of adoption is measured and whether new roles, responsibilities and authorities have been permanently reflected in the organizational structure and processes.","keyRisks":["Users not adopting new systems and processes and reverting to old methods or informal workarounds","Incorrect application of new controls due to inadequate training","The change being left without an owner and senior management support fading over time"],"practices":[
{"code":"BAI05.01","name":"Establish the desire to change.","title":"Making the case for change and assessing readiness","expectation":"For significant transformations, the scope of the change, the affected groups and their level of readiness for change are analyzed. The rationale for the change is set out with concrete evidence such as a business problem, opportunity or risk; potential resistance and actions to reduce it are identified. The results of the analysis provide input to the program plan and the communication plan.","design":"Assess whether the change management approach defines, as determined by the organization, the size of change for which impact and readiness analysis is mandatory.","operating":"For selected significant transformation initiatives during the period, inspect whether the impact and readiness analysis was performed at the start of the initiative and whether the identified actions were carried into plans.","evidence":["Change impact analysis","Anonymized stakeholder readiness survey results","Resistance points and action plan","Management presentations setting out the rationale for change"],"findings":["In a large-scale ERP migration, the affected user groups and their readiness levels were not analyzed.","A readiness survey was conducted, but no actions were planned based on its results."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.02","name":"Form an effective implementation team.","title":"Setting up a cross-functional change team with clear roles","expectation":"The team driving the change consists of representatives from business units, IT, human resources and, where necessary, security and compliance; members’ roles, authorities and time commitment are defined in writing. Shared team objectives and success measures are defined. The change sponsor has the authority to support the team’s decisions.","design":"Assess whether the team structure represents all affected functions and whether roles, decision rights and success measures are defined.","operating":"For selected change initiatives, inspect team assignments, meeting records and objective tracking to verify that the team operates as defined.","evidence":["Change team appointment letter or organization chart","Role and responsibility definitions (RACI)","Team objectives and success measures","Team meeting records"],"findings":["The change team does not include a representative from the most affected business unit.","Change activities were postponed because the time team members were to devote to the initiative was not defined."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.03","name":"Communicate desired vision.","title":"Communicating the vision for change to the target audience","expectation":"The purpose of the change, its expected benefits and its impact on employees are communicated in a message owned by senior management and expressed in language the target audience understands. The communication plan defines audiences, messages, channels, timing and responsibilities, and provides for collecting feedback. Messages are updated according to the stages of the change.","design":"Assess whether the communication plan is tailored to different user groups and whether it includes senior management involvement and a feedback mechanism.","operating":"For selected initiatives, verify through records that planned communication activities such as announcements, meetings and intranet content took place and that feedback was evaluated.","evidence":["Change communication plan","Senior management announcements and presentations","Records of completed communication activities","Feedback and Q&A records"],"findings":["Communication was carried out only by the technical team through general e-mails; there is no message from senior management.","User feedback was collected but was not evaluated or responded to."],"iso":[],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.04","name":"Empower role players and identify short-term wins.","title":"Equipping role holders and making early wins visible","expectation":"Responsibilities are formally assigned to the individuals who will take on roles in new processes; the training, tools and authorities they need are provided, and the organizational structure and job descriptions are updated accordingly. Segregation of duties and least privilege principles are observed when assigning authorities. Concrete short-term wins are identified, measured and shared to build confidence in the change.","design":"Assess whether a process exists to ensure that role assignments are reflected in job descriptions and access rights, and whether training needs are identified on a role basis.","operating":"For selected new roles, inspect through read-only access reports whether the job description, training record and system access are consistent with one another and that no segregation of duties conflicts exist.","evidence":["Updated job descriptions and organization chart","Role-based training plan and attendance records","Access rights reports for new roles","Early win measurements and communication records"],"findings":["Users assigned to the approver role in the new process were granted system access without having received training.","Because job descriptions were not updated, the new responsibilities have not been formally assumed."],"iso":["6.3","5.3"],"clauses":["7.2"],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.05","name":"Enable operation and use.","title":"Preparing users and operations to run and use the new solution","expectation":"Before go-live, user guides, operations and support procedures, training, support team readiness and intensive support arrangements for the transition period are completed. Users and operations teams have the knowledge and tools they need to fulfill their responsibilities. Readiness is assessed using a checklist before the go-live decision.","design":"Assess whether the go-live readiness checklist covers training, documentation, support and handover to operations.","operating":"For selected significant solutions deployed to production during the period, inspect the readiness checklist, training attendance status and whether operations procedures were published before go-live.","evidence":["Go-live readiness checklist","User and operations documentation","Training attendance records","Handover to the support team and transition period support plan"],"findings":["Operations procedures were prepared after go-live; incidents in the first weeks were managed without documentation.","A significant portion of users obtained system access without attending training."],"iso":["5.37","6.3"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.06","name":"Embed new approaches.","title":"Embedding new ways of working and retiring old ones","expectation":"The level of adoption of implemented changes is monitored through usage measures, process performance indicators and user feedback. Use of legacy systems, old methods or informal workarounds such as parallel spreadsheets is identified and discontinued in a controlled manner. Policies, procedures and performance targets are updated to reflect the new way of working.","design":"Assess whether indicators measuring adoption are defined and whether the retirement of old methods and access is planned.","operating":"For selected initiatives, inspect usage and adoption reports; verify through read-only access lists and process records that legacy system access and parallel processes have been closed.","evidence":["Adoption and usage measurement reports","Updated policies and procedures","Records of legacy system decommissioning and access removal","User feedback analysis"],"findings":["Although the new system has been deployed, users continue to track transactions in parallel spreadsheets.","Because access to the legacy application was not removed, data is being updated inconsistently in two systems."],"iso":["5.18"],"clauses":[],"itgc":""},
{"code":"BAI05.07","name":"Sustain changes.","title":"Sustaining the change over time","expectation":"Training programs are maintained for new joiners and employees changing roles; the outcomes of the change are monitored regularly by senior management and successes are shared. Adoption and compliance are reviewed at intervals defined by the organization, and corrective action is taken for deviations. Knowledge gained and lessons learned are recorded for future changes.","design":"Assess whether onboarding and periodic training programs cover the new processes and whether an ownership and review mechanism exists to monitor sustainability.","operating":"From the list of employees who joined or changed roles during the period, verify through records that selected individuals completed the relevant training and that planned adoption reviews were performed.","evidence":["Onboarding and periodic training content","Training completion records for new employees","Adoption review reports and management minutes","Lessons learned log"],"findings":["Training on the new process was not added to the onboarding program, so new employees follow the old method.","The level of adoption of the change has not been measured at all since project closure."],"iso":["6.3"],"clauses":["7.2"],"itgc":""}
]}
]
);
